{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>At the appointed time I will return to you.</b> From here is a hint to what the Sages, blessed be their memory, held: that on Pesach Isaac was born — למוע\"ד in gematria equals בפסח." "<b>At the time of life.</b> Rashi explained: at this time, that you will be alive next year — since it does not call it 'at the time of life' [in the sense of] the time when a woman gives birth, which is nine months. And 'life' is used of a woman giving birth, as we say: three keys were not handed over to an agent — that of the revival of the dead, that of rain, and that of חיה [childbirth]. Now, since it says 'at the time' to mean that you are alive and well — and it seems to me that Rashi's position follows the position of Seder HaOlam and the Mekhilta, for thus it is stated there: on the fifteenth of Nisan the Holy One, blessed be He, spoke with Abraham; on the fifteenth of Nisan the ministering angels came to announce to Sarah; on the fifteenth of Nisan Isaac was born. And we also say in Bereshit Rabbah: ״לושי ועשי עוגות״ — this teaches that it was Pesach. And so it implies in the passage of circumcision above, where it is written: ״אשר תלד לך שרה למועד הזה בשנה האחרת״. However, all this disputes the first chapter of Rosh HaShanah, which concludes that ״למועד אשוב אליך״ refers to the festival of Sukkot, and [explains] ״למועד״ — the first festival that will be, which is Pesach. And also [it disputes] Pirkei deRabbi Eliezer, [which says] that on Yom HaKippurim our father Abraham was circumcised; and in the chapter השוכר את הפועלים we say that on the third day after his circumcision the angels came to him, and then Sarah was announced. And that which we say in Rosh HaShanah that it refers to the festival — [it means] two days before the festival. And furthermore, in Rosh HaShanah we also conclude: on Rosh HaShanah Sarah, Rachel, and Hannah were remembered — and this follows the position of Rashi, of blessed memory, here. And if you ask according to the position there: what is ״למועד הזה בשנה האחרת״ — since it refers to Sukkot and He tells him [of] Pesach, if so it was in the same year? One may answer that he holds like Rabbi Joshua, who says: in Nisan the world was created, and therefore he calls Pesach 'the next year.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:05:39.263037450Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}